'\" t
.TH AS01 1 "October 2026" "mc6801-tools" "mc6801-tools Manual"
.SH NAME
as01 \- assembler for the Motorola MC6801/MC6803
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B as01
.RB [ \-gkuw ]
.RB [ \-b
.IR binfile ]
.RB [ \-l
.RI [ listfile ]]
.RB [ \-m
.RI [ listfile ]]
.RB [ \-n
.IR name ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR objfile ]
.RB [ \-s
.IR symfile ]
.RB [ \-t
.IR n ]
.RI [ srcfile ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B as01
assembles one MC6801 source file.
It writes a relocatable object file in Introl linkable binary format
for
.BR ld01 (1),
an absolute binary image, or both.
It can also write a listing and a symbol table.
.PP
The assembler makes two passes over the source.
If
.I srcfile
is omitted, it reads standard input.
Options and the source file may appear in any order.
Each option must be a separate argument, and each output file may be
given only once.
.PP
If there are errors, the object and binary files are deleted
(unless
.B \-k
is given) and the exit status is 1.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-b " binfile"
Write an absolute binary image to
.IR binfile .
Use
.B ORG
to set the load address.
The image starts at the lowest address assembled and ends at the
highest; any gaps between
.B ORG
areas are filled with zeros.
.BR IMPORT ,
.B .GLOBL
and other external references are not allowed in this mode.
.TP
.B \-g
Put only global (exported and imported) symbols in the object file.
.TP
.B \-k
Keep the object and binary files even if there are errors.
.TP
.BR \-l " [\fIlistfile\fP]"
Write a listing to
.IR listfile ,
or to standard output if no file name follows.
.TP
.BR \-m " [\fIlistfile\fP]"
List every line of each macro expansion, including directives and
lines skipped by conditionals.
On its own,
.B \-m
lists only the macro expansions; use
.B \-l \-m
.I listfile
to get the full listing as well.
.TP
.BI \-n " name"
Use
.I name
to derive the module name stored in the object file.
By default the source file name is used.
Any directory part and the last suffix are removed.
.TP
.BI \-o " objfile"
Write a relocatable object file to
.IR objfile .
.TP
.BI \-s " symfile"
Write a symbol table to
.IR symfile .
.TP
.BI \-t " n"
Assemble
.B .TEXT
sections into location counter
.IR n +3
instead of location counter 0.
.TP
.B \-u
Treat undefined symbols as imported, as if each had been named in an
.B IMPORT
directive.
.TP
.B \-w
Disable warnings.
This is the default; use
.B \-w\-
to enable them.
.TP
.B \-v
Print a version message and exit.
.PP
The flags
.BR \-g ,
.B \-u
and
.B \-w
are turned off by appending
.BR \- ,
for example
.BR \-w\- .
.SH SOURCE FORMAT
Each line has the form
.PP
.RS
.RI [ label [ \fB:\fP ]]
.RI [ operation
.RI [ operands ]]
.RB [ ;\c
.IR comment ]
.RE
.PP
A label without a colon must start in column 1.
A label followed by a colon may be indented.
An operation must be preceded by white space unless it follows a label.
A line whose first character is
.BR * ,
.B ;
or
.B !
is a comment.
A
.B ;
or
.B !
starts a comment anywhere else on the line.
.PP
Symbols may contain letters, digits,
.B _
and
.BR . ,
and must not start with a digit.
They are case sensitive.
Mnemonics, directives and register names are recognized in all upper
case or all lower case.
.SS Constants
.TS
l l.
\fB123\fP	decimal
\fB$7F\fP, \fB0x7F\fP	hexadecimal
\fB0177\fP	octal (leading zero)
\fB%1010\fP	binary
\fB\(aqA\fP or \fB\(aqA\(aq\fP	character
.TE
.SS Expressions
From lowest to highest precedence:
.TS
l l.
\fB=  <  >\fP	comparison (true is \-1, false is 0)
\fB+  \-  |\fP	add, subtract, bitwise or
\fB*  /  &  <<  >>\fP	multiply, divide, and, shifts
\fB\-  +  ~\fP	unary minus, plus, complement
.TE
.PP
Parentheses group subexpressions.
A
.B *
in operand position is the current location counter.
.SS Addressing modes
.TS
l l l.
Inherent	\fBinx\fP
Immediate	\fBldaa #$10\fP	\fB#\fP prefix
Direct	\fBldaa $80\fP	address in $00\-$FF, chosen automatically
Extended	\fBldaa $1234\fP
Indexed	\fBldaa 5,x\fP	unsigned 8\-bit offset; \fB,x\fP means \fB0,x\fP
Relative	\fBbne loop\fP	branches only
.TE
.PP
A
.B <
before an operand forces direct addressing and a
.B >
forces extended.
Direct addressing is chosen only when the address is known in pass 1,
so forward references use extended addressing.
The read\-modify\-write instructions
.RB ( NEG ,
.BR COM ,
.BR LSR ,
.BR ROR ,
.BR ASR ,
.BR ASL / LSL ,
.BR ROL ,
.BR DEC ,
.BR INC ,
.BR TST ,
.BR CLR )
and
.B JMP
have no direct mode on the MC6801 and always use extended
for non\-indexed operands.
.SH DIRECTIVES
Alternative spellings are separated by
.BR / .
.SS Data
.TP
.BR FCB / .BYTE / DB / .DATA1 " \fIexpr\fP[\fB,\fP\fIexpr\fP...]"
Define bytes.
.TP
.BR FDB / .WORD / DW / .SHORT / .DATA2 " \fIexpr\fP[\fB,\fP\fIexpr\fP...]"
Define 16\-bit words (big\-endian).
.TP
.BR FCC / .ASCII " \fIdelim text delim\fP"
Define a string.
The first character is the delimiter.
C escape sequences such as
.B \en
and
.B \e0
are recognized.
.TP
.BI .ASCIZ " delim text delim"
Like
.BR FCC ,
followed by a zero byte.
.TP
.BR RMB / .BLKB / .SPACE " \fIcount\fP[\fB,\fP\fIfill\fP]"
Reserve
.I count
bytes, filled with
.I fill
if it is given.
.TP
.BR .BLKW / .ZEROW " \fIcount\fP"
Reserve
.I count
words.
.SS Symbols
.TP
.IB label " EQU " expr \fRor\fP " label " = " expr"
Define a constant.
.TP
.IB label " SET " expr
Define a symbol that may be redefined.
.TP
.BR EXPORT / PUBLIC / .DEFINE " \fIsym\fP[\fB,\fP\fIsym\fP...]"
Make symbols defined in this module visible to other modules.
.TP
.BI ENTRY " sym"
Like
.BR EXPORT ,
and also mark
.I sym
as an entry point.
The linker uses the first entry point as the program's start address.
.TP
.BR IMPORT / EXTERN / .EXTERN / EXTRN " \fIsym\fP[\fB,\fP\fIsym\fP...]"
Declare symbols defined in another module.
.TP
.BR .GLOBL / .GLOBAL " \fIsym\fP[\fB,\fP\fIsym\fP...]"
Export the symbols if they are defined in this module, otherwise import
them.
.TP
.IB label " COMM " size \fRor\fP " label " LCOMM " size"
Define a common block of
.I size
bytes.
.B LCOMM
makes it local to the module.
.TP
.BR .COMM / .LCOMM " \fIname\fP\fB,\fP\fIsize\fP"
The same, in Unix assembler syntax.
.SS Location
.TP
.BI LOC " n"
Switch to location counter
.I n
(0 to 15).
The linker places counters 0 and 1 in the text segment and the others in
the data segment.
.TP
.BR .TEXT ", " .DATA ", " .ROM ", " .BSS
Switch to location counter 0
.RB ( .TEXT )
or 3 (the others).
.TP
.BR .SECT " \fIname\fP"
Switch to the named section
.RB ( .text ,
.B .data
or
.BR .bss ).
.TP
.BI ORG " expr"
Set the location counter.
.TP
.BI BLOCK " expr \fRand\fP " ENDB
Assemble the lines in between at
.IR expr ,
then restore the previous location counter.
.TP
.BI .ALIGN " n"
Advance the location counter to a multiple of
.IR n .
.TP
.B .EVEN
Advance the location counter to an even address.
.SS Conditional assembly
.TP
.BI IF " expr \fR/\fP " ELSEIF " expr \fR/\fP " ELSE " \fR/\fP " ENDIF
Assemble lines selectively; a non\-zero expression is true.
.TP
.BI IFC " (str1,str2) \fR/\fP " ELSEIFC " (str1,str2)"
True if the two strings are identical.
.SS Macros
.TP
.BI MACRO " name \fR...\fP " MEND
Define a macro.
In the body,
.BR ?1 ,
.BR ?2 ,
\&...
are replaced by the comma\-separated arguments of the invocation.
Invoke it as
.I name
.IR arg1 , arg2 ...
.SS Other
.TP
.BR INCLUDE / GET " \fIfile\fP"
Assemble the contents of
.IR file .
.TP
.B END
End of source; the rest of the file is ignored.
.TP
.B FAIL
Report an error.
.TP
.BR .LIST ", " .MACLIST " [\fIn\fP]"
Adjust the listing and macro\-listing counters.
They only have an effect when listing is enabled with
.B \-l
or
.BR \-m .
.TP
.BR .WARN " [\fIn\fP]"
Add
.I n
(default \-1) to the warning counter.
Warnings are shown while the counter is 0 or more.
It starts at \-1, or at 0 with
.BR \-w\- ,
so
.B .WARN 1
turns warnings on and
.B .WARN
turns them off again.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I objfile
Introl linkable binary object (magic bytes
.BR S1 ).
.SH EXAMPLES
Assemble a module and produce a listing on standard output:
.PP
.RS
.B as01 \-o screen.o \-l screen.s
.RE
.PP
Assemble an absolute image with no linking step:
.PP
.RS
.B as01 \-b prog.bin prog.s
.RE
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Errors and warnings are shown in the listing under the offending line,
with a caret marking the position.
A summary of the error and warning counts is printed at the end.
If no listing file was requested, the offending lines and the summary are
printed on standard output.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ld01 (1),
.BR ar01 (1),
.BR objdump01 (1),
.BR objcopy01 (1)
.PP
The mc6801\-tools User\(aqs Manual
.RI ( mc6801\-tools\-manual.pdf ).
.SH HISTORY
